.TH XYMOND_LOCATOR 8 "Version 4.3.30:  4 Sep 2019" "Xymon"
.SH NAME
xymond_locator \- Xymon service locator for distributed worker modules
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "xymond_locator [options]"

.SH DESCRIPTION
xymond_locator lets the work of one Xymon installation be spread over several
servers: for instance, two servers each holding the RRD files for half of the
monitored hosts. Worker modules running on those servers register with the
locator, and the programs that need a worker for some host ask the locator
which server handles that host.

The locator is a small directory service. It keeps a list of servers per
service type, and a list of which host is assigned to which server. It does
not handle any monitoring data itself.

It answers requests on a UDP socket, by default port 1984 on all interfaces.
Note that this is UDP, while
.I xymond(8)
listens on TCP port 1984; both can run on the same host.

The locator is not needed, and not started by the default
.I tasks.cfg(5),
on a Xymon installation where one server does everything.

.SH OPTIONS
.IP "\fB--listen\fR=\fIIP\fR[:\fIPORT\fR]"
The address and UDP port to listen on. Default: 0.0.0.0:1984. If the port is
omitted, 1984 is used.

.IP "\fB--daemon\fR"
Run as a stand-alone background process. The default is to stay in the
foreground, as a task started by
.I xymonlaunch(8)
expects.

.IP "\fB--no-daemon\fR"
Stay in the foreground. This is the default.

.IP "\fB--logfile\fR=\fIFILENAME\fR"
Redirect output to this log file. See
.B BUGS
for what a HUP signal does.

.IP "\fB--debug\fR"
Log every request received and the decisions taken.

.SH SERVICE TYPES
Each server registers for one service type, and each lookup names one:
.br
"rrd" \- RRD files, handled by
.I xymond_rrd(8)
.br
"hostdata" \- host data snapshots, handled by
.I xymond_hostdata(8)
.br
"client" \- client data analysis, handled by
.I xymond_client(8)
.br
"alert" \- alerting, handled by
.I xymond_alert(8)
.br
"history" \- known to the locator, but no worker module registers for it.

.SH HOW A SERVER IS CHOSEN
Every server registers with a weight, and the weight decides what it receives:

.IP "weight 2 or more"
The server is up and accepts any host. New hosts are shared round-robin
between such servers, each server taking (weight \- 1) hosts per round: two
servers with weights 3 and 2 receive new hosts in the ratio 2:1.

.IP "weight 1"
The server is up, but accepts only hosts already assigned to it. It receives no
new hosts. This is the default weight of a worker module, so a worker meant to
take new hosts must be given \fB--locatorweight\fR=2 or more.

.IP "weight 0"
The server is down.

.IP "negative weight"
Only one server receives requests: the one with the lowest weight. If it goes
down, the next lowest takes over. Two servers with weights \-2 and \-1 form an
active/standby pair.

.I xymond_alert(8)
uses this mode, but always registers with weight \-1, whatever
\fB--locatorweight\fR says. Among several alert servers the weights are then
equal, and the active one is the first by name in text order, ignoring case:
10.0.0.10:1986 comes before 10.0.0.9:1986.

.PP
A service is either sticky or roaming. For a \fBsticky\fR service ("rrd" and
"hostdata"), a host is assigned to the first server chosen for it and stays
there, so that its files are kept in one place. For a \fBroaming\fR service
("client"), each request may go to a different server.

When a server goes down, the hosts assigned to it are not moved: requests for
them are refused until the server comes back, so that a host's files are not
split between two servers. Data for those hosts is dropped meanwhile. A host is
released, and assigned anew, only when its server is removed from the locator
with a "forget" request.

A sticky worker module tells the locator which hosts it already holds data for
when it starts, so a restarted server gets its hosts back.

.SH WHAT IS SPREAD, AND WHAT IS REDUNDANT
The locator spreads the work of the worker modules. It does not spread or
duplicate
.I xymond(8)
itself: one xymond still receives every status and builds the web pages, and
when it stops, so does everything else. The locator is itself one process.

What losing a worker server costs depends on the service type:

.IP "rrd, hostdata"
The data is spread, not copied. Each host's files are on one server, and while
that server is down the data for its hosts is dropped; no other server takes
over. Graphs and snapshots for a host are served by the server holding them,
which is what \fB--locatorextra\fR is for. This spreads disk load on a large
installation, and protects against nothing.

.IP "client"
The load is spread, and the service survives the loss of a server: hosts roam,
so the remaining servers take the reports of the one that is down.

.IP "alert"
Nothing is spread: one server sends all the alerts. The others are standby, and
one of them takes over when the active server goes down.

.PP
To keep monitoring through the loss of the xymond server, the locator is not
the tool. Run two complete Xymon servers, and have every client report to both
by setting XYMSRV to 0.0.0.0 and listing both servers in XYMSERVERS; see
.I xymon(1).

.SH SETTING UP DISTRIBUTED WORKERS
On each server that is to handle part of the work, run the worker module on
its own, listening on the network instead of reading from
.I xymond_channel(8):
.IP
xymond_rrd \-\-rrddir=$XYMONVAR/rrd \-\-listen=10.0.0.2:1985
\-\-locator=10.0.0.1:1984 \-\-locatorweight=2
.PP
The worker registers with the locator under its \fB--listen\fR address, and
accepts messages on that TCP port. The worker options are:

.IP "\fB--listen\fR=\fIIP:PORT\fR"
Where the worker accepts messages. Required for network mode.

.IP "\fB--locator\fR=\fIIP:PORT\fR"
Where xymond_locator listens. Required for network mode.

.IP "\fB--locatorid\fR=\fIIP:PORT\fR"
The name the worker registers under. Default: the \fB--listen\fR address. It
must be the address other servers can reach the worker on.

.IP "\fB--locatorweight\fR=\fIN\fR"
The weight to register with, see
.B HOW A SERVER IS CHOSEN.
Default: 1. Ignored by
.I xymond_alert(8),
which always registers with \-1.

.IP "\fB--locatorextra\fR=\fITEXT\fR"
Extra information returned with lookups. The web CGIs use it as the URL of the
CGI directory on that server, to send a browser to the server holding a host's
data.

.PP
On the server running
.I xymond(8),
the channel is fed to the locator instead of a local worker:
.IP
xymond_channel \-\-channel=status \-\-locator=10.0.0.1:1984 \-\-service=rrd
.PP
For each message, xymond_channel asks the locator which server handles the
host, and forwards the message to that server over TCP. If the connection to a
server fails, xymond_channel reports the server as down to the locator. While a
server is unreachable, only the newest message for it is kept; earlier ones are
discarded.

A worker module that is stopped normally reports itself as down.

A worker on a server of its own still needs what it would find on the server
running
.I xymond(8).
.I xymond_alert(8)
loads the current state of all statuses from xymond when it starts handling
messages, and gives up without it: set XYMSRV in that server's
.I xymonserver.cfg(5)
to the xymond server. It also alerts only for hosts in its own
.I hosts.cfg(5),
so give it the same hosts.cfg as the xymond server.

.SH EXAMPLES
The locator runs on 10.0.0.1, xymond on 10.0.0.2. Each program below runs as a
task in the
.I tasks.cfg(5)
of its own server.

.B "RRD files on two servers."
On the locator host:
.IP
.nf
[locator]
	ENVFILE /usr/local/xymon/server/etc/xymonserver.cfg
	CMD xymond_locator \-\-listen=10.0.0.1:1984
.fi
.PP
On each RRD server, 10.0.0.3 and 10.0.0.4, without xymond_channel:
.IP
.nf
[rrdworker]
	ENVFILE /usr/local/xymon/server/etc/xymonserver.cfg
	CMD xymond_rrd \-\-rrddir=$XYMONVAR/rrd \-\-listen=10.0.0.3:1985
	    \-\-locator=10.0.0.1:1984 \-\-locatorweight=2
.fi
.PP
On the xymond server, the [rrdstatus] task feeds the locator instead of a
local xymond_rrd:
.IP
.nf
[rrdstatus]
	ENVFILE /usr/local/xymon/server/etc/xymonserver.cfg
	NEEDS xymond
	CMD xymond_channel \-\-channel=status
	    \-\-log=$XYMONSERVERLOGS/rrd\-status.log
	    \-\-locator=10.0.0.1:1984 \-\-service=rrd
.fi
.PP
Each host's RRD files are then kept on one of the two servers. Open UDP port
1984 on the locator host, and TCP port 1985 on the RRD servers.

.B "Alerts from an active and a standby server."
On each alert server, 10.0.0.5 and 10.0.0.6, set XYMSRV in
.I xymonserver.cfg(5)
to 10.0.0.2, install the same
.I hosts.cfg(5)
and
.I alerts.cfg(5)
as on the xymond server, and run:
.IP
.nf
[alertworker]
	ENVFILE /usr/local/xymon/server/etc/xymonserver.cfg
	CMD xymond_alert \-\-listen=10.0.0.5:1986 \-\-locator=10.0.0.1:1984
	    \-\-checkpoint\-file=$XYMONTMP/alert.chk
.fi
.PP
On the xymond server:
.IP
.nf
[alert]
	ENVFILE /usr/local/xymon/server/etc/xymonserver.cfg
	NEEDS xymond
	CMD xymond_channel \-\-channel=page
	    \-\-log=$XYMONSERVERLOGS/alert.log
	    \-\-locator=10.0.0.1:1984 \-\-service=alert
.fi
.PP
10.0.0.5:1986 comes first by name, so 10.0.0.5 sends the alerts. When it
stops, 10.0.0.6 takes over; alerts already sent by 10.0.0.5 may then be sent
again, since the standby does not know about them.

.B "Client data analysed on two servers."
On each analysis server, 10.0.0.7 and 10.0.0.8, set XYMSRV in
.I xymonserver.cfg(5)
to 10.0.0.2: the worker loads hosts.cfg from that xymond, and sends the
statuses it computes (cpu, disk, memory and the rest) back to it. Then run:
.IP
.nf
[clientworker]
	ENVFILE /usr/local/xymon/server/etc/xymonserver.cfg
	CMD xymond_client \-\-listen=10.0.0.7:1987 \-\-locator=10.0.0.1:1984
	    \-\-locatorweight=2
.fi
.PP
On the xymond server, the [clientdata] task feeds the locator instead of a
local xymond_client:
.IP
.nf
[clientdata]
	ENVFILE /usr/local/xymon/server/etc/xymonserver.cfg
	NEEDS xymond
	CMD xymond_channel \-\-channel=client
	    \-\-log=$XYMONSERVERLOGS/clientdata.log
	    \-\-locator=10.0.0.1:1984 \-\-service=client
.fi
.PP
The "client" service roams, so each client report goes to whichever server is
next; neither keeps any data.

A CMD line is written on one line in tasks.cfg; it is wrapped here for
reading.

.SH FILES
.IP "$XYMONTMP/locator.servers.chk"
.IP "$XYMONTMP/locator.hosts.chk"
The registered servers, and the hosts assigned to them. Read at start-up, and
written when the locator terminates or receives a "save state" request. If
XYMONTMP is not set, /tmp is used.

.SH SIGNALS
.IP "HUP"
Re-open the log file, then terminate; see
.B BUGS.
.IP "TERM"
Save the state files and terminate.

.SH BUGS
A HUP signal is meant only to re-open the log file, but it also interrupts the
wait for the next request, which the locator treats as a fatal error: it logs
"select error, aborting: Interrupted system call", saves its state files and
exits. Started by
.I xymonlaunch(8),
it is then restarted, and requests sent meanwhile go unanswered.

.SH NOTES
The interactive \fBlocator\fR program built in the lib/ directory of the
source tree can send any request to the locator, including registering,
downing and forgetting servers, and querying a host. It is not installed by
"make install".

.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymond(8), xymond_channel(8), xymond_rrd(8), xymond_hostdata(8),
xymond_client(8), xymond_alert(8), xymon(7)
